00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.880
All within a single click.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.640
And ready to resume
from the next time.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.590
And resume isn't just for media.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.920
Let me show you one
other partner use case.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.760
A user is texting a friend on
WhatsApp for shopping items.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.580
In the mid-conversation,
they just sent a bunch

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:43.260
of links to be looked at.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.030
When they sit down at the PC,

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.340
WhatsApp published
a resume context.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.710
Windows received it and
the badge shows up.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.990
The same conversation
gets picked up right

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.990
where they left off on
their desktop app now.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:56.730
Music continuity,
chat continuity

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.020
or document continuity.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.340
You define what resume means for
your app and Windows routes it.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.680
So how does this all
work under the hood?

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.950
There are two ways to
integrate Windows resume.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:12.000
Let me hand it to Avinash to
talk you through both of them.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.770
AVINASH NOWDURU: Thanks, Sai.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:17.040
As we discussed, resume
supports two integration paths.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.400
One is continuity SDK and
the other one is Windows

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.870
push notifications.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.930
The right choice depends on your
platform, connectivity model

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.760
and the engagement scenario.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.630
Let's quickly walk through how
each integration path works.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.610
Let's explore the first
integration path,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.410
continuity SDK.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.840
Here's the end-to-end
flow, how it works.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.090
The app publishes a
resumable activity

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.460
through the continuity
SDK on Android.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.400
Windows receives it through
the LTW CDL signaling

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.480
and it surfaces on taskbar in
Windows as a resume entry point.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.100
When the user clicks resume,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:53.160
Windows activates the desktop
app using the app-provided deep

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:57.030
link, restoring the experience
exactly where the user left off.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.030
Let's see how you can
achieve it with your app.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.510
Step 1, add a dependency
to your Gradle file.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:07.380
Continuity SDK is now
published to Maven and GitHub.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.960
Step 2, declare in your manifest

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.720
that your app is a resume
activity provider.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.260
That value 4 maps
to resume activity.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:21.180
Step 3, initialize the SDK and
register your event handlers.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.550
You get two responses back.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:26.370
OnContextRequestReceived,
which means it indicates

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:27.960
that connection is established.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.980
The app can then send
ResumeAppContext to LTW.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.790
OnSyncServiceDisconnected
is your signal to stop.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.100
You get this if the
device is disconnected

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.200
or the sync channel goes down.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.980
And then you call
AppContextManager.Initialize.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.380
From this point onwards,
your app is wired

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.700
into the continuity platform.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.680
Step 4, create an
AppContext object,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.320
which represents
a resume intent.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.900
ContextId here uniquely
identifies this

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.520
activity instance.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.410
Type Resume Activity.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.390
This line is the important one.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.970
This tells Windows that the
payload should participate

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:09.480
in cross-device
resume experiences.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.730
Finally, send AppContext.

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:14.880
This publishes the activity
into the continuity platform.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.090
Step 5, handle callbacks
from the platform.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:21.450
OnContextResponseSuccess
confirms the resume activity is

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:24.630
successfully accepted by LTW.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.690
OnContextResponseError
tells you something failed.

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:29.220
Maybe a connectivity dropped

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.800
or the sync service
became unavailable.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.980
The important pattern
here is resiliency.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:37.440
Apps should stop publishing
while disconnected

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.080
and retry once the
connection is restored.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.690
Let's explore the
second path, WNS,

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:44.700
Windows Notification Service.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:48.270
This doesn't require links to
Windows or the continuity SDK.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.700
Your app's cloud backend
communicate directly

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:52.770
to the Windows using
the WNS channel

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:54.480
through push notifications.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.480
It surfaces on taskbar
as a resume entry point.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.070
When the user
clicks on "Resume",

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.740
Windows activates the desktop
app restoring the experience

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.810
exactly where the user left off.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.690
The trade-off, no link
to Windows needed,

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.600
works for any platform
like Android or iOS,

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.060
but the app must be
installed on Windows.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.900
There's no store installation
flow on this path.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.720
Let's see how you can achieve
this within your app.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.510
The first step, register
your channel URA.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.610
Your PC requests for
a WNS channel URA

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.320
and sends it to the server.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.720
The channel URA becomes the
address for communicating

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:30.060
with Windows from your app.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:35.190
Build the resume request and
send the WNS notification.

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:37.860
Here's a sample code
how you can do that.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.680
Some mandatory headers
while sending a request.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.510
The decision matrix.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.940
Pick the path that
fits your scenario.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:47.940
Deep mobile to PC integration
with installation flow.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.860
Continuity SDK is your route.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:53.490
Broad reach, cloud driven,
app already on Windows.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.410
WNS should be your choice.

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.510
Both paths give you
the same result.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:01.290
A taskbar badge, a deep link,
and a user experience that picks

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:03.840
up right where they left off.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.700
Resume is a limited
access feature.

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:08.100
You'll need to request
access through Microsoft.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.420
And here are the links for that.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:15.180
Back to you, Sai.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:15.930
SAI TEJASWY
MYLAVARAPU: All right.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.960
That was about continuity
of app activity.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.050
Now let's talk about
people in Windows.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.600
App contacts can become
a first-class citizens

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.430
across Windows.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:29.610
They have a potential to
get showed on widgets

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.960
and even the search box.

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:33.990
All right.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.210
Let's say you are an
application like WhatsApp

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:38.640
and you have integrated
with the People API.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.280
You've donated your
users people to Windows

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.190
to become first class
citizens in the user system.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:45.360
Here's what happens.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.610
This is the People widget
in the widgets port.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:48.810
See those contacts?

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.060
Every one of them that
came from WhatsApp.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.850
WhatsApp donated these
contacts using the People API

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:55.920
and Windows is
showing them here.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:57.330
Now notice the order.

00:08:57.330 --> 00:08:59.820
Some take first position
and some later.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:00.930
That's not random.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:04.140
That's because WhatsApp also
donated interaction signals.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:05.880
This helps in ranking.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:10.260
You as an app feed the signals
and Windows handles the math.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:12.930
Now watch what happens when
I click on this contact.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:15.300
One click and WhatsApp
opened directly

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.010
that particular conversation.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:19.920
That happened because WhatsApp
registered a communication URI

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.630
for that contact.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:25.130
Whenever the user clicked,
when the user clicked

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.280
that particular chat,
Windows fired that URI

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:29.630
and launched the chat.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:30.680
And here's the thing.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:34.250
WhatsApp didn't write a single
line of widget UI code here.

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:36.240
The people API
handled everything.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.820
Now let me show you a
similar integration

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.910
in a different surface for
a different user intent.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:45.150
The user is looking for someone
in the Windows system search.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.190
And when your app has
donated the contacts

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:50.850
with the right signals, Windows
can match that for the user.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.380
There is a contact
user is looking

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:55.170
for right alongside apps, files,

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:58.050
and even web results
as a people result.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:00.780
You can see WhatsApp
attribution right there.

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:04.950
And look, the action buttons,
message, call, and video call.

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:09.030
WhatsApp donated contacts once
and it shows up here in search

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.100
with actions ready to go.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.650
Now, how do you
actually build this?

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.750
Let me hand it over
to Avinash again

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.670
to walk you through the code.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:18.930
AVINASH NOWDURU: Thank you, Sai.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.200
Let me show you what this
looks like in a code.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:25.410
I'm going to walk you through
full integration from zero

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:29.520
to contacts showing up in
widgets, search, and share.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.800
Start by creating a
user data account.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.620
Third-party apps are required
to create a user data account

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:37.410
with
com.microsoft.peoplecontract.

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:41.100
That's your opt-in to
that people platform.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.740
This is a one-time
setup per app.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.080
Next, storing contacts.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:49.290
Create a contact list and
set other app read access

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:50.640
to system only.

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.800
That last line is
very important.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:56.820
It means only Windows system
services like widget, search,

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.280
share can read these contacts.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.740
No other app on the
device can see them.

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:04.860
Your user's contact
data stays controlled.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.920
Next, create a contact object.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.620
The remote ID here is your
app's unique identifier

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:11.970
for that contact.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.290
And then save it.

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:15.840
Windows now knows
about this contact.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:19.740
These contacts become eligible
as share suggestions, widgets,

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:21.840
and search experiences.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:24.060
This is where it
gets interesting.

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:27.120
So far, app has only
donated a contact.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.750
Now, we enrich that contact
with capabilities, deep links,

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:33.240
and interaction semantics.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.420
Annotations are how you tell
Windows what action your app

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.700
supports for this contact.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:42.840
The first way of enriching a
contact is through interactions.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:45.390
Teaching Windows what
action your app supports

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:46.950
for that contact.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:50.730
This is where communication URL
annotations come into picture.

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:53.970
You declare the operation your
app supports for a contact,

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:57.360
like message, audio
call, video call.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:00.570
For each operation, you register
a corresponding protocol

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:02.280
in your app manifest.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:04.380
Windows surfaces
enable interaction

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:07.320
with contacts directly through
apps' communication capabilities

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:09.360
using these protocols.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:12.630
The next layer of enriching
a contact is relevance,

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:15.060
helping Windows understand
which contacts matter

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.250
to the user right now.

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:18.780
Donating signals.

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:20.640
Signals are lightweight
interaction events

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.230
with your app contacts.

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:25.080
Your app donates signals
without any PII.

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.450
And Windows uses
these signals to rank

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:29.550
and surface the most
relevant contacts

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:32.790
across widgets,
search, and share.

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:33.990
Back to you, Sai.

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:36.300
SAI TEJASWY MYLAVARAPU: Thank you
for the quick walkthrough, Avinash.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:39.570
The key idea of Connected
Experiences API isn't just

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.100
one-time integration.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:42.810
That's a value compounding.

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.360
The real mode for
the apps as part

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:48.120
of Connected Experiences
platform is,

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:52.020
the more your app donates about
users, people, and activities,

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:54.480
it will drive improved
engagement back

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:55.560
to your application.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:58.890
And which will also result
in more number of downloads

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:03.360
and users building a trust to
your desktop applications.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:07.710
And all throughout this,
users' data stays in control.

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:10.980
The contacts are scoped
to system surfaces only,

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:13.830
no app-to-app sharing,
and everything is removed

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:15.750
on uninstall of the app.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:18.510
Users can toggle it to
control the visibility

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:21.420
of these contacts anytime
within their settings.

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:24.690
All of this is built into
the platform by design.

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.160
Here's how you get started.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:29.220
For people, integrate with
the cross-device people API.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:32.760
Donate your contacts,
add communication URLs,

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:36.540
and donate activity signals
so your contacts rank higher

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:39.540
across widget,
search, and share.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:44.550
For resume, integrate with
either Continuity SDK or WNS,

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:48.210
and sometimes even both to
make your app's activity work

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:52.740
across Android as well as
iOS apps with Windows.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:56.760
Start with learn.microsoft.com
links that are presented here

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:58.890
for each of these areas.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:02.370
Connected Experiences is how
your app not just be an app

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:06.000
on Windows, but actually
become a part of Windows.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:09.810
You write the integration once
and Windows gives you surfaces,

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:13.530
ranking, engagement,
and installs.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.780
Thank you and enjoy the
rest of the sessions.
